[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe for loading a **single-file `.pmtiles` archive** into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`) that makes a `pmtiles://` URL work with MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why only once?**  
> `addProtocol` registers a global handler. Registering it inside a React component’s render loop, for example, will create duplicate handlers and can leak memory.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – `pmtilesProtocol.tile` already contains the compatibility shim that works with the new promise-based handler signature, so you don’t have to write any `async (params, abort)` code yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### Vector tiles (the most common case)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // One source per .pmtiles file
      world: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The protocol prefix tells the handler to treat the URL as a PMTiles file
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/planet-earth.pmtiles'
        // No `tiles:` array – the protocol reads the archive header and returns a TileJSON
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw water from the "water" layer inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // Example: draw roads from the "road" layer
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1
        }
      }
      // …add as many layers as you need, each with its own `source-layer`
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### Raster tiles (PNG/JPEG imagery)

```js
{
  sources: {
    imagery: {
      type: 'raster',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/satellite.pmtiles',
      tileSize: 256          // optional, defaults to 256
    }
  },
  layers: [
    {
      id: 'sat',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'imagery',
      paint: {}
    }
  ]
}
```

### Raster-DEM (terrain) tiles

```js
{
  sources: {
    terrain: {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation.pmtiles',
      encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox"
    }
  },
  terrain: {
    source: 'terrain',
    exaggeration: 1.5
  }
}
```

---

## 4️⃣ Host the `.pmtiles` file where it can be fetched with **HTTP Range** requests

| Provider | How to enable |
|----------|---------------|
| **AWS S3** | Public read or signed URL; S3 already supports `Range`. Set `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000` and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. |
| **Cloudflare R2** | Same as S3 (S3-compatible API). Put behind Cloudflare CDN for extra caching. |
| **GitHub Pages** | Works for files ≤ 100 MiB. Enable CORS via a `CNAME` or `*.github.io` origin (GitHub adds `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`). |
| **Netlify / Vercel / any static host** | Verify that the server returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. Add the CORS header if it isn’t automatic. |

> **CORS is required** – browsers will block the range request unless the host sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (and usually `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`).  

> **Do NOT gzip the whole file** – the archive is already compressed per-tile. If the server applies a second gzip layer, the byte offsets become wrong and range requests will fail.

---

## 5️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features) | You used `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` instead of `url:` | Switch to `url:` – the protocol reads the archive header and supplies the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`. |
| **404 / 403** on tile fetch | CORS header missing or the file is not publicly readable | Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and make the object public. |
| **“Tile fetch error: 416”** | Server does not support `Range` requests | Move the file to a host that does (S3, R2, Netlify, etc.). |
| **MapLibre v4+ throws “data is undefined”** | You wrote a custom protocol handler that returns a raw `ArrayBuffer` | Handlers must resolve to `{data: <ArrayBuffer|Blob|Uint8Array>}`. Using `pmtilesProtocol.tile` avoids this entirely. |
| **Zoom levels above the archive’s max return empty tiles** | You referenced the archive with `tiles:` instead of `url:` (MapLibre assumes maxzoom = 22) | Use `url:`; the protocol will over-zoom automatically. |
| **Large file (> 100 MiB) fails on GitHub Pages** | GitHub Pages size limit | Host the file on S3/R2 or split the archive with `pmtiles extract`. |

---

## 6️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/planet-earth.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: {
            'line-color': '#777',
            'line-width': 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://example.com/planet-earth.pmtiles` with the URL of your own archive.*

